  "body": {
    "id": "prop:hybrid-expected-risk-upper",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "There is a universal constant \\(C<\\infty\\) such that, for every \\(n,m\\ge2\\), the hybrid estimator satisfies \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H}\\in\\mathfrak E_{n,m}\\) and\n\\[\n \\sup_{P\\in\\mathfrak P^{\\mathrm{pred}}_{n,m}(r,\\mu,K,q,B,\\lambda)}\n E_P\\!\\left[\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H},U)\\right]\n \\le C\\bigl(1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\bigr).\n\\]",
    "free_symbols": [
      "UhatH",
      "Eobs",
      "n",
      "m",
      "P",
      "r",
      "mu",
      "K",
      "q",
      "B",
      "lambda",
      "L",
      "U",
      "aloose"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:minimax-hybrid-estimator",
      "def:observed-estimators",
      "def:predictable-class",
      "def:rowwise-loss",
      "lem:deterministic-sine-fallback",
      "prop:ipw-expected-risk-upper"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The quantity \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\) is deterministic and strictly positive because \\(n,m\\ge2\\), \\(r\\ge1\\), \\(\\mu\\ge1\\), \\(B>0\\), \\(q>0\\), and \\(\\lambda_{nm}>0\\).  The recorded score is a measurable function of \\((A,Y,p)\\), and rank projection followed by the fixed tie rule is measurable; hence \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I}\\in\\mathfrak E_{n,m}\\) by \\(\\mathrm{def:observed\\mbox{-}estimators}\\).  Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:deterministic\\mbox{-}sine\\mbox{-}fallback}\\) gives \\(Q^{\\mathrm D}\\in\\mathfrak E_{n,m}\\).  Selection between these two estimators by the deterministic event \\(\\{a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\le1\\}\\) preserves observed-data measurability and orthonormality, proving \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H}\\in\\mathfrak E_{n,m}\\).\n\nIf \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\\le1\\), the hybrid definition gives \\(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H}=\\widehat U^{\\mathrm I}\\), and Proposition \\(\\mathrm{prop:ipw\\mbox{-}expected\\mbox{-}risk\\mbox{-}upper}\\) yields\n\\[\n \\sup_P E_P\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H},U)\n \\le C_1a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}\n =C_1(1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}).\n\\]\nIf \\(a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}>1\\), the hybrid definition and the pathwise sine-frame inequality give\n\\[\n \\sup_P E_P\\mathcal L(\\widehat U^{\\mathrm H},U)\n \\le1+\\sqrt{2/\\mu}\n \\le1+\\sqrt2\n =(1+\\sqrt2)(1\\wedge a^{\\mathrm{loose}}_{nm}),\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\mu\\ge1\\) was used in the second inequality.  Taking\n\\(C=\\max\\{C_1,1+\\sqrt2\\}\\) proves the uniform assertion in both cases.",
    "justification": "The deterministic sine frame adds the missing uniform elbow to the concrete observed-data upper construction.",
    "gap": "At fixed complexity and bounded aspect ratio, bounded-outcome nonemptiness makes the hybrid eventually choose its constant-risk branch; whether another estimator has vanishing uniform risk is open.",
    "consumer": "The hybrid gives an explicit admissible estimator for the minimax upper endpoint without adding exogeneity or factor predictability.",
